  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively connect temperature-dependent HEA mechanical behavior with dislocation activity, lattice distortion, stacking faults, FCC–HCP structural changes, modulus loss, and high-temperature softening. R5 additionally establishes temperature-dependent tribological testing of a refractory HEA film, while R17 shows a temperature threshold for recovery/recrystallization-driven softening. However, none describes nanoindentation-and-friction simulation of an FCC FeNiCrCoCu coating on Cu, nor the specific sequence in which dislocation density first falls with an modulus increase near 600 K and then rapid lattice disorder above about 900 K causes sharp hardness/modulus loss and increased friction. Thus the literature points in the right mechanistic direction, but selecting the particular coated system, simulation protocol, and combined disorder-to-friction mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
